= Choosing our enemies

In our media and campaign materials, who should we consider as our main opponents?

[? if enemies != 0 : Previously, we had considered the [+ enemies +] as our prior enemies. Is a change of direction necessary? ?]

- @anti_dem: The anti-democratic parties - KPD, DNVP, alongside many other left and right wing extremists.
- @right: The Right - The DNVP shall be our main opponents
- @nsdap: The Volkisch alone.
- @kpd: The KPD.
- @socialist: The SPD and other socialist movements.
- @easy_discard

@anti_dem
on-arrival: enemies = "anti-democratic parties"; soclib_strength += 5; socrep_strength += 5; liberal_dissent += 5; new_middle_nsdap -= 2; new_middle_dnvp -= 2; dnvp_relation -= 10

Attacking all the non-republican parties is generally popular in our party, however, some on the right dislike our alienation of the DNVP.

@right
on-arrival: enemies = "DNVP"; soclib_strength += 7; soclib_dissent -= 5; liberal_dissent += 7; industrial_dissent += 7; ddp_relation += 7; new_middle_nsdap -= 3; new_middle_dnvp -= 3

Attacking the DNVP is strongly supported by the parties left wing, however, the right is concerned about our hesitancy to attack the socialists and our continued alienation of the DNVP. The SPD is happy with us though.
@nsdap
on-arrival: enemies = "Volkisch"; nazi_urgency += 1; new_middle_nsdap -= 4;

The Volkisch are currently weakened, however attacking them is still popular with the entire party.
@kpd
on-arrival: enemies = "KPD"; uspd_relation -= 5;

Attacking the KPD has universal support in the party.

@socialist
on-arrival: enemies = "Socialists"; liberal_strength += 8; liberal_dissent -= 5; dvp_relation += 7; dnvp_relation += 7; uspd_relation -= 10; ddp_relation -= 10; soclib_dissent += 8; new_middle_ddp -= 3 

The DNVP and DVP appreciate our targeting of the socialists, however, the left of our party and the SPD are not happy with this whatsoever.
